[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
**Short answer:** Yes – the `font-faces` block you use in a MapLibre Native style can be copied verbatim into a MapLibre GL JS style and it will give you the same “offline-font” behaviour, **provided** the browser can actually fetch the font files (or they are already cached).  

Below is a step-by-step checklist, the gotchas that are specific to the web, and a minimal example you can drop into a GL JS app.

---

## 1. What `font-faces` does in GL JS

| Feature | Native | GL JS (≥ 6.7.0) |
|---------|--------|----------------|
| Reads the `font-faces` property from the style JSON | ✅ (Android 11.13+, iOS 6.18+) | ✅ (since 6.7.0) |
| Loads the font files **through the browser** (via `<link>`/`@font-face` or `fetch`) | ✅ (reads from the device’s file system) | ✅ (must be an HTTP(S) URL the page can reach) |
| Honors `unicode-range` per file | ✅ | ✅ |
| Uses the files for shaping complex scripts (Arabic, Devanagari, etc.) | ✅ (via HarfBuzz) | ✅ (via the browser’s text engine) |
| Can be changed at runtime with `map.setFontFaces()` | – | ✅ |

So the **data model** is identical – you only need to make the URLs browser-friendly.

---

## 2. Checklist for copying the block

| ✅ Item | Why it matters | How to satisfy it |
|--------|----------------|-------------------|
| **URL accessibility** | GL JS fetches the font with `fetch()`; a `file://` path or a native-only `android_asset://` URL will fail. | Host the `.ttf/.otf` files on the same origin as your web app (or on a CORS-enabled CDN). Relative URLs like `"fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf"` work great. |
| **CORS headers** (if cross-origin) | Browsers block font loading without `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. | Serve with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |
| **Cache for offline** | “Offline-font” means the browser must have the file already. | Use a Service Worker (or the `Cache` API) to pre-cache the font files **and** the style JSON. |
| **`glyphs` field** | GL JS will still try to load glyph PBFs for any script that is *not* covered by a `font-faces` entry. | • If you want *pure* local-font mode, either (a) omit `glyphs` entirely (GL JS ≥ 5.11.0 will fall back to TinySDF for Latin and to the browser for complex scripts) **or** (b) keep a dummy `glyphs` URL that points to an empty server (won’t be hit for covered scripts). |
| **`localIdeographFontFamily`** (CJK) | CJK characters are rendered locally by default; they ignore `text-font`. | No extra work needed unless you want a specific on-device CJK font – then set `localIdeographFontFamily` to the family name you have in `font-faces`. |
| **Version** | The `font-faces` property is ignored in GL JS < 6.7.0. | Make sure you load `maplibre-gl-js` ≥ 6.7.0 (current stable is 6.15+). |

---

## 3. Minimal example

**style.json (excerpt)**

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": "https://example.com/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",   // can be omitted if you want pure local mode
  "font-faces": {
    "Noto Sans Regular": [
      {
        "url": "fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0000-00FF", "U+0100-024F"]   // Latin + Latin-Extended-A
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Devanagari Regular": [
      {
        "url": "fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0900-097F"]
      }
    ],
    "Noto Sans Arabic Regular": [
      {
        "url": "fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf",
        "unicode-range": ["U+0600-06FF"]
      }
    ]
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": [
          "Noto Sans Devanagari Regular",
          "Noto Sans Arabic Regular",
          "Noto Sans Regular"
        ],
        "text-size": 14
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**index.html (service-worker registration for offline)**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre offline fonts</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.15.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';

// Register the service worker (must be served over HTTPS or localhost)
if ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {
  navigator.serviceWorker.register('sw.js');
}

// Create the map – note we *omit* `glyphs` if you truly want local-only mode
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'style.json',          // the JSON above, served from the same origin
  center: [77.2, 28.6],
  zoom: 5,
  // optional: change CJK font family
  // localIdeographFontFamily: '"Noto Sans CJK SC", sans-serif'
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**sw.js (very simple cache-first strategy)**

```js
const CACHE_NAME = 'offline-fonts-v1';
const ASSETS = [
  '/',                     // index.html
  '/style.json',
  '/fonts/NotoSans-Regular.ttf',
  '/fonts/NotoSansDevanagari-Regular.ttf',
  '/fonts/NotoSansArabic-Regular.ttf',
  // add any sprite / source tiles you want offline as well
];

self.addEventListener('install', e => {
  e.waitUntil(caches.open(CACHE_NAME).then(cache => cache.addAll(ASSETS)));
});

self.addEventListener('fetch', e => {
  e.respondWith(
    caches.match(e.request).then(resp => resp || fetch(e.request))
  );
});
```

With the Service Worker in place, the browser will have the font files cached *before* the map tries to render any label, so the app works completely offline – exactly the same experience you get on Native.

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Labels appear in a generic system font** | The URL in `font-faces` returns 404 or is blocked by CORS. | Open the URL directly in the browser → 200 OK. Add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header if cross-origin. |
| **Only part of a word is styled** (e.g., Devanagari letters appear in one font, vowel signs in another) | The `unicode-range` does not cover the full grapheme cluster. | Expand the range to include the combining marks (`U+0900-097F` already does for Devanagari). |
| **Arabic text is still left-to-right** | Using a GL JS version < 6.9.0 *and* not loading the RTL plugin. | Upgrade to ≥ 6.9.0 (no plugin needed) **or** load the plugin as shown in the skill. |
| **CJK characters look blocky** | `localIdeographFontFamily` is set to `false` (or you served CJK glyph PBFs). | Remove the `localIdeographFontFamily: false` line or set it to a nicer on-device font family you have in `font-faces`. |
| **Map breaks when offline** | The style JSON itself is not cached. | Add `style.json` (and any sprite files) to the Service Worker cache list. |

---

## 5. TL;DR for your exact scenario

1. **Copy the `font-faces` block** from your Native stylesheet into the GL JS style JSON *unchanged* (the syntax is identical).  
2. **Make sure the `url` values point to files the browser can fetch** (relative to the page or a CORS-enabled CDN).  
3. **Cache those `.ttf/.otf` files** (Service Worker, Workbox, or the browser’s “offline” mode) so they are available when the user is offline.  
4. **Optionally omit `glyphs`** if you want *pure* local-font rendering; otherwise keep it – GL JS will only fall back to it for scripts you haven’t covered with `font-faces`.  
5. **Test on a fresh browser session** (clear caches) to verify the fonts load without any network request.  

If you follow those steps, the GL JS web app will render labels exactly the same way your MapLibre Native app does, using the same offline-font files and Unicode-range slicing. 🎉
